From 15c9d83fcc8c8d223ec4fe9e64ffdf66300d61da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 01:55:38 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] microblaze: add 64-bit constants with addi and addic

adddi3's operand 2 has the predicate arith_operand32, which accepts a
register or a constant from 0x80000000 to 0xffffffff, while its
immediate alternatives have the constraints P (1 to 65535) and N
(-65535 to -1).  No constant satisfies both, so the immediate
alternatives were dead: x + 1 loaded 0 and 1 into a register pair and
added it with add and addc, six instructions with the moves around
them, and a constant the predicate did accept was reloaded into a pair
all the same.

Accept any CONST_INT and add it a word at a time: addi adds the low
word and sets the carry, then addic adds the high word and the carry,
or addc rD, rA, r0 when the high word is zero.  The assembler gives
each word an imm prefix when it does not fit in 16 signed bits.  When
the low word is zero there is no carry, and the high word is a plain
addik.  The old N alternative also had a third instruction, an addi of
-1, that addic makes unnecessary.  A one-word overlap of the
destination and the source goes through r18, as in adddi3_carry,
though with double words in even register pairs it cannot happen.
Subtracting a constant reaches adddi3 as an addition of its negation.

x + 1 is now addi and addc; x - 3 and x - 0x8000 are addi and addic of
-1; x + 0x80000000 is imm, addi and addc; x + 0x100000000 is one addik.
The text of newlib's libc.a, built by the compiler, shrinks from
419733 to 419521 bytes and libgcc.a from 75624 to 75524.  Compiling
gcc.c-torture/execute and gcc.target/microblaze, 1645 files, changes 27
of them at -O2 and 26 at -Os, all smaller, by 916 and 884 bytes.  In
loops the constant was usually already hoisted into registers, so the
instruction counts of a 64-bit counting loop barely move (110030 to
110022 for 10000 iterations).

others/adddi3-imm-run.c adds 23 constants at the 16-bit, 32-bit and
carry boundaries, and subtracts them, from 21 values including low
words of 0xfffffffe and 0xffffffff, and compares each result with a
register add.

Tested on microblaze-elf and microblazeel-elf under QEMU:
gcc.c-torture/execute.exp and gcc.target/microblaze, with no new
failures.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

